.TH APACHETOP 1 "September 2026" "apachetop 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
apachetop - Apache HTTP Server scoreboard monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B apachetop
.RB [ -f " " PATH ]
.RB [ -i " " SEC ]
.RB [ -t " " SEC ]
.RB [ -n " " ROWS ]
.RB [ -s " " SORT ]
.RB [ -a ]
.RB [ -A ]
.RB [ -1 ]
.RB [ -r ]
.RB [ -d ]
.RB [ -v ]
.br
.B apachetop --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B apachetop
is a read-only terminal monitor for the Apache HTTP Server scoreboard.
It reads the scoreboard directly from the APR shared-memory object instead
of requesting
.B /server-status
over HTTP.

This is useful on overloaded Apache installations where
.B /server-status
may be unavailable because all Apache workers are busy serving requests.
The program can inspect the shared-memory scoreboard without requiring
another HTTP request to be served by Apache.

The program uses Apache's own
.B scoreboard.h
structures and derives the shared-memory layout from the installed Apache
headers rather than duplicating the scoreboard structure definitions.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Apache must make its scoreboard available through a shared-memory file.
The Apache configuration must have
.B mod_status
loaded,
.B ExtendedStatus On
enabled, and a
.B ScoreboardFile
setting pointing to the scoreboard file.

For example:
.PP
.nf
LoadModule status_module /usr/lib/apache2/modules/mod_status.so
ExtendedStatus On
ScoreboardFile /dev/shm/scoreboard
.fi
.PP
The exact
.B LoadModule
path depends on the distribution and package layout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -f " PATH"
Use the specified scoreboard file instead of automatically searching
.B /dev/shm/ShM.* .
.TP
.BI -i " SEC"
Set the refresh interval in seconds. The default is 1.0 seconds.
Values below 0.1 seconds are clamped to 0.1 seconds.
.TP
.BI -t " SEC"
Alias for
.BR -i .
.TP
.BI -n " ROWS"
Set the maximum number of rows displayed in each section.
Without this option the number of rows is fitted automatically to the
terminal height.
.TP
.BI -s " SORT"
Sort the main worker table. Supported values are:
.BR slot ,
.BR age ,
.BR client
(or
.BR ip ),
and
.BR vhost
(or
.BR host ).
The default is
.BR slot .
With
.BR age ,
newest workers are shown first.
.TP
.B -a
Show only active workers.
.TP
.B -A
Show all scoreboard slots, including unused or dead slots with PID 0.
.TP
.B -1
Display one snapshot and exit.
.TP
.B -r
Show the recent/last-request section in addition to the main worker table.
.TP
.B -d
Show calculated Apache/APR scoreboard layout information.
.TP
.B -v
Print the application version and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Print the application version and exit.
.TP
.B -h
Show usage information.
.SH DISPLAY
The main table includes the scoreboard slot, process ID, worker state,
worker age, client address, virtual host, and current request.

The status characters include:
.PP
.nf
R  reading the request from the client
W  processing/writing a response
K  keepalive
L  logging
D  DNS lookup
C  closing
G  graceful
_  ready/idle worker
.fi
.PP
The header also shows aggregate request and byte counters, request rate,
byte rate, and the number of active workers.

Unused
.B SERVER_DEAD
slots with PID 0 are hidden by default. Use
.B -A
to include them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/shm/ShM.*
Apache APR shared-memory scoreboard objects that are automatically detected
when no explicit path is supplied.
.TP
.B /dev/shm/scoreboard
Example path for an Apache
.B ScoreboardFile
configuration. The actual path is determined by the Apache configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B apachetop
Automatically find the scoreboard and refresh every second.
.TP
.B apachetop -i 2
Refresh every two seconds.
.TP
.B apachetop -a
Show only active workers.
.TP
.B apachetop -A
Show all scoreboard slots.
.TP
.B apachetop -s age
Sort workers by age, newest first.
.TP
.B apachetop -r
Show recent/last requests.
.TP
.B apachetop -1
Print one snapshot and exit.
.TP
.B apachetop -f /dev/shm/scoreboard
Use an explicitly configured scoreboard file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful operation or a successful
.B -v ,
.B --version ,
or
.B -h
operation.
.TP
.B 1
No usable scoreboard was found or the scoreboard could not be opened.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH NOTES
The scoreboard is a live state interface, not an access log. It does not
provide historical per-URL statistics.

If no scoreboard can be found automatically, verify that Apache's
configuration contains a valid
.B ScoreboardFile
setting and that the configured file is readable by the user running
.BR apachetop .
.PP
The Debian/Ubuntu packages are built for the distributions listed in the
project documentation and install this manual page as
.BR apachetop (1).
